We study how employment-protection institutions shape the first decade of
workers' careers. Using linked employer–employee data and a reform that changed
dismissal costs for a subset of firms, we show that stronger protection slows
early-career mobility and compresses wage growth, with the largest effects for
workers who enter during downturns.
